; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The technical export: what one deployment hands over when somebody asks for material rather than
; for a verdict. A receipt attests a response, a pack attests that the responses inside a window are
; all of them, and this container attests nothing of either kind. It carries originals, it says who
; assembled the carrying and what they assert about it, and it states in one place whether any legal
; assessment was made of what is inside. That is a document a customer can hand to a lawyer and an
; auditor can carry out of a deployment, and it is a different kind of thing from a pack, so it is a
; different container: its own content type, its own version, its own refusals.
;
; Two differences make a new pack version the wrong instrument, and they are the whole reason this
; file exists beside `pack.cddl` rather than as a `v: 2` inside it. The first is that an export has to
; be able to say "no legal assessment was made" as a fact about itself, which pack v1 cannot express
; because its duty block is mandatory and every one of its members states a period, a revision and a
; span. Absence is not a value a pack can hold, and a pack that carried no duty would be a pack whose
; reader could not tell "no duty applies" from "the writer forgot the block". The second is that an
; export carries arbitrary originals: a contract, a screenshot, an export of a ticket, a receipt. Pack
; v1 carries receipts and nothing else, because its walk hashes the exact receipt bytes of every item
; and its span bounds every one of their stamps. Those are two different jobs done by two different
; documents, and a reader must never be able to confuse them, which is what the content type is for.
;
; `packages/receipt/src/export.ts` reads this layout and refuses what is refused below by code, and
; `docs/export-v1.md` is the document a reimplementer reads. The framing a chained export walks is
; section 5.2 of `docs/receipt-spec.md`, published as byte images in
; `packages/fixtures/data/chain-v1.json`; where a sentence here and those two disagree about a frame,
; they are the authority and this file is wrong.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashaveri-Export = #6.18(COSE_Sign1-Export-COSE)

; The receipt's envelope and the pack's, unchanged but for the content type: COSE_Sign1 per RFC 9052
; section 4.2, encoded per RFC 8949 with Core Deterministic Encoding. Determinism matters here more
; than it does in a pack, because an export is opened by somebody who did not build it, often in a
; proceeding: a reader who rebuilds a document from the manifest inside it and the originals beside it
; has to get the bytes back out whole, or cannot tell a handover that was edited from one whose reader
; re-encoded it.
COSE_Sign1-Export-COSE = [
    protected: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Export-Protected-Header,
    unprotected: { * any => any }, ; the map that carries no claim; see the note below the manifest
    payload: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Export-Manifest,
    signature: bstr .size 64
]

; Three labels, the same three the receipt and the pack sign with, and a typ that differs from both.
; The typ is not a label for a reader's convenience: one deployment key signs receipts, packs and
; exports, and a reader that took an export for a pack would verify a signature over a manifest that
; states no duty and no span, then report it as an answer for a window. Nothing downstream recovers
; from that, because both documents pass their own checks. So each of the three says which of the
; three it is, at the first bytes, before anything is read out of the payload.
Ashaveri-Export-Protected-Header = {
    1: -8,                       ; alg: EdDSA (Ed25519)
    4: bstr .size 32,            ; kid: sha256(public_key)
    3: "ashaveri/export",        ; typ
}

; The signed manifest. Four members beside the version, and each one earns its place the way pack's do:
; by being something a reader of this container needs that nothing inside the container states.
;
; `assessment` is the member this container exists to add, and the block carrying it is where it is
; stated. `collection` is the material and its shape, empty or not. `claim` is the writer's own
; assertion about
; where the material came from, which a reader verifies by reading who signed it and nothing else. `at`
; is the assembly instant, and it is the only clock in this document: an export states no window, so it
; bounds no stamps and answers for no period, and what `at` fixes is the moment the bytes below were
; gathered, which is what a later reader compares a claim's own stamp against.
;
; `v: 1` is one version, and it says what the fields attest rather than which software wrote them, as
; it does in `receipt.cddl` and `pack.cddl`. A second version arrives the day a reader would draw a
; different conclusion from the same bytes. An assessment block that gained a second label, a claim that
; gained a checked proof, a collection that gained a third shape: each of those is that day, and none of
; them is this file's business. What is this file's business is that a reader of `v: 1` refuses the
; other day's bytes rather than reading them as this day's, which is what the closed maps below and the
; one-label assessment both do.
Ashaveri-Export-Manifest = {
    v: 1,                        ; format version
    at: int,                     ; unix seconds, the instant the export was assembled
    assessment: ExportAssessment,
    collection: ExportCollection,
    claim: ExportClaim,
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The assessment, which is the reason this is a second contract.
;
; An export is made for a purpose a deployment may not share, and often it is made precisely so that
; somebody else can form a view. A deployment that has decided nothing about which legal category its
; material falls into must be able to say so, and it must not be able to say so by saying nothing. That
; is the whole design of this block: it is required, its one label is the statement, and its sentence is
; the writer's own words. A reader that meets an absent `assessment` refuses the document as malformed
; rather than reading it as an export that assessed nothing, and that distinction is the difference
; between a handover and a silence.
;
; No legal vocabulary is defined here, and that is not a gap for somebody to fill in later in this
; version. There is no article label, no duty name, no category, no qualification, no period, no
; mapping from a system to an obligation. Two reasons, and both are load-bearing. The first is
; competence: whether a per-request receipt is a log a named article requires turns on the system and
; the actor, and this estate has said plainly elsewhere that it does not interpret those questions; a
; format that enumerated answers would be an answer. The second is that the statement has to survive
; being made by a writer who is wrong. "No legal assessment was made" is checkable by a reader against
; this document, which carries no assessment; "this material is not subject to a duty" is not, and the
; second is the kind of sentence a container should not carry under its own signature as though the
; signature established it.
;
; So `k` has one value in `v: 1`, and `states` carries the writer's sentence, bounded so that it cannot
; be empty. The sentence is not checked by a reader and cannot be: it is what the writer says it is
; stating. What is checked is that the position is filled, that the label is the one this version
; defines, and that a document which assessed something could not arrive wearing this one. A later
; version may define a second label, and the format does not forbid it: a version that did would name
; what a reader owes the claim, and a reader of `v: 1` meeting that label refuses it by code rather than
; guessing which of the two it found.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExportAssessment = {
    k: "none",                   ; the only assessment statement this version defines
    states: tstr .size (1..256)  ; the writer's own sentence stating that no assessment was made
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The collection: the material, and the shape of the claim being made about it. Three arms, chosen by
; `k`, and each closed. A reader learns whether it is being shown a chained run, an unstructured set of
; originals, or an explicit statement that there is nothing to show, from the first member of this map
; rather than by counting an array and inferring.
;
; The arms are three because the questions are three. "Here are receipts from a run, and you can walk
; them" is one claim. "Here are files somebody asked for, and no ordering or completeness is asserted
; about them" is another, and it is a thing a deployment genuinely wants to say without lying. "There
; is nothing here, and this is why" is the third. Collapsing the second into the first would make every
; export promise a chain it may not have; collapsing the third into the second would make emptiness a
; silence, which is what this container exists to refuse.
;
; An empty collection has an explicit outcome, and that decision is the opposite of pack v1's on
; purpose. A pack of nothing has no first node, so its walk is vacuous and its span would read as a
; window that held nothing, which is a statement about retention and belongs to a retention artifact. An
; export of nothing is a real and useful answer to a real request: an auditor asks for everything held
; for a period, and "nothing was carried out of this store, and here is the reason the writer gives" is
; the honest reply. So `ExportVoidCollection` is a shape a conforming export may take, and it states its
; emptiness in a sentence that cannot be empty. What it may not do is read as success. Two rules make
; that concrete, and a conforming reader enforces both. The first is that an item array under a
; non-void arm is never empty: `[+ …]`, so a writer cannot reach the void statement by leaving the
; array out of the shape that needs it. The second is that a reader's answer for a void collection
; carries no items to report on and no walk to have completed: it reports the emptiness as the outcome,
; and a caller cannot print a count of verified material it was not given.
;
; `ExportAnchoredCollection` carries the two endpoints a walk runs between, exactly as `PackChain` does
; and for the same reason: neither is derivable from the items, an anchor a reader invented would let a
; deployment start the walk wherever it liked, and a head read off the last item handed over would let it
; stop there. The walk block at the end of this file states what a completed walk does and does not
; establish.
;
; `ExportPlainCollection` carries no endpoints, and that is the point of the arm: an export of a
; customer's own contract, or of three screenshots about a ticket, has no run to claim and must not be
; able to imply one by leaving two digests out of a map. Absence is a shape here, not an omission: the
; arm names itself, and the arm that would have carried the digests is the one a reader would look for.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExportCollection = ExportAnchoredCollection / ExportPlainCollection / ExportVoidCollection

ExportAnchoredCollection = {
    k: "anchored",               ; a chained run is claimed over these items
    anchor: bstr .size 32,       ; the digest the first item was chained from
    head: bstr .size 32,         ; the digest of the last item in the run
    items: [+ ExportChainedItem] ; at least one; the void arm is where nothing lives
}

ExportPlainCollection = {
    k: "plain",                  ; originals, with no ordering or completeness claimed
    items: [+ ExportItem]        ; at least one, for the same reason
}

ExportVoidCollection = {
    k: "void",                   ; nothing is carried, and this arm is how that is said
    states: tstr .size (1..256)  ; the writer's own sentence stating the outcome
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One item: a name, a stamp, a digest, optionally a place in a chain, and the original itself.
;
; `id` is the writer's name for the item and no two items in one collection carry the same one, which
; is a rule about a reader's report rather than about storage: a refusal names the item it stopped at,
; and two items answering to one name make that report mean whichever of the two a reader happened to
; meet first. The ceiling is the framing's own, since the walk hashes the id through a two-byte length;
; a plain export could carry a longer name, and refusing it at the same width both arms can reach is
; cheaper than one rule that differs per arm.
;
; `iat` is the stamp the writer records for the original, and this container asks nothing of it beyond
; being a whole number of seconds no earlier than the epoch: an export states no span, so there is no
; window here for a stamp to fall outside. In an anchored item it is also a term of the hashed input, so
; a receipt restamped into a run it was never chained in breaks a link rather than slipping through.
;
; `d` is sha256 of the exact original bytes, whole, whatever those bytes are and whether they travel
; inline or sit beside the document under the name the companion arm gives. It is the member that makes
; an export checkable at all: the signature covers the manifest, the manifest names a digest, and the
; digest is of the material, so one byte altered anywhere between the export's assembly and its reading
; is `EXPORT_DIGEST_MISMATCH` rather than a handover nobody noticed. A companion reference is verified
; the same way against whatever bytes the reader was handed for that name; it is never verified against
; nothing, which is what the reader's separate refusal for an absent companion says.
;
; `p`, in an anchored item, is the digest of the record before it, or the collection's anchor for the
; first item. It is published rather than derived because the walk follows links and not positions:
; retiring a prefix and compacting a file rewrite the front of a chain without touching a surviving
; record's digest, and an ordinal would state a second time an order the bytes already fix while staying
; free to disagree with it. What the link buys with it is the half of the rule an endpoint cannot see,
; which is stated under the walk below.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExportItem = {
    id: tstr .size (1..65535),  ; the writer's name for this item, unique in the collection
    iat: int,                   ; unix seconds, the stamp recorded for the original
    d: bstr .size 32,           ; sha256 of the exact original bytes
    orig: ExportOriginal         ; the bytes themselves, or where they sit beside this document
}

ExportChainedItem = {
    id: tstr .size (1..65535),  ; as above
    iat: int,                   ; unix seconds, and a term of the hashed input
    d: bstr .size 32,           ; sha256 of the exact original bytes
    p: bstr .size 32,           ; the digest this record was chained from, or the anchor for the first
    orig: ExportOriginal        ; the signed receipt bytes, whole and unaltered
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The original, in one of two shapes. A handover that carried only inline bytes could not leave a
; gigabyte behind, and a handover that carried only references could be checked against nothing, so the
; two are alternatives and the item says which it is.
;
; `inline` states that the bytes are in this document and are exactly these, which is the strongest form
; a reader can verify without a second file. `companion` states that the bytes are the ones travelling
; under this name, alongside this document, and the item's digest is what binds the name to the
; material: a reader handed a companion file hashes it and compares, and a reader handed none refuses
; that item by name instead of reporting a set of material it never looked at.
;
; A companion name is a name and not a location. It carries no URL, no path that climbs out of the
; directory the export arrived in, and no scheme, because this format publishes no resolution rule and a
; reader that invented one would be fetching evidence over a network the deployment chose not to open.
; The character set is what a portable file name is made of on every system this estate ships to; the
; width is a file name's, not a path's.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExportOriginal = ExportInlineOriginal / ExportCompanionOriginal

ExportInlineOriginal = {
    k: "inline",                 ; the bytes are inside this document
    bytes: bstr                  ; the exact original bytes, whole
}

ExportCompanionOriginal = {
    k: "companion",              ; the bytes travel beside this document
    name: tstr .size (1..255)    ; the file name they travel under
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The claim: the writer's own statement about where this material came from and what was done to it, and
; nothing a reader verifies. It is in the signature, so a reader can say that this is what the holder of
; this key asserted, and that is the whole of what the signature establishes about it. Every other
; container in this estate is built so that a reader can recompute its claims; this one carries a block
; that cannot be recomputed, because provenance and custody are facts about a process rather than about
; bytes, and the alternative to stating them is leaving a reader to infer them from the absence of a
; statement.
;
; So the block is required, its kind is chosen from two labels this version can mean, and its sentence is
; bounded but free. `by` is who makes the assertion, named by the assertor rather than resolved to an
; identity a reader could look up; `made` is when, and it is bounded against `at` rather than against a
; clock the reader owns, because a claim stamped after the assembly of the document carrying it is a
; different document's claim and this one cannot mean it. `kind` is which of the two assertions is being
; made: `provenance` about where the material came from, `custody` about who has held it since. A label
; outside those two is refused with its own code rather than read as whichever of the two a reader felt
; it resembled, which is the confusion this block would otherwise import.
;
; Nothing here is a legal category, and the two labels are not one: they name what the writer says about
; the material's history, which is the writer's to say, and not what the material is under any body of
; law, which is the question the assessment block exists to leave open.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ExportClaim = {
    made: int,                   ; unix seconds, when the assertion was made, never after `at`
    by: tstr .size (1..256),     ; who asserts it, as they name themselves
    kind: "provenance" / "custody", ; which of the two assertions this is
    states: tstr .size (1..2048) ; the assertion, in the writer's own words
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The walk, and its bound. An anchored collection claims that its items are records of one chain, so a
; reader walks them: start at the item whose `p` is the anchor, recompute each record's digest as
; section 5.2 of `docs/receipt-spec.md` frames it, sha256 over 0x00 || p || iat as eight big-endian
; bytes || the byte length of id as two big-endian bytes || id || the original bytes, and require that
; the next item's `p` names the digest just recomputed. The walk is complete when a recomputed digest is
; the head and exactly one item ends the run there.
;
; Four things it refuses, and a conforming reader refuses all four: a gap, where a record names a
; predecessor nobody in the collection is; a duplicate, where two items claim one predecessor or answer
; to one name; an unreachable item, one the walk never visits; and a head it cannot reach, where the run
; stops short of the signed endpoint. The first three are what the links see, and the last is what the
; signed endpoint is for. The check has two halves and a reader that implements one has implemented half
; a rule: the walk from the anchor to the head, and the count of what that walk reached against the array
; it was handed. A receipt parked beside a run it is not part of reaches the head exactly as the honest
; ones do, because the walk stops where the head is and never counts what it did not need to visit, so
; the second half is the only one with eyes for it.
;
; What a completed walk does not establish is the part a reimplementer is most likely to over-read, and
; it is stated here rather than in prose about a field. It is not proof that the source is complete. A
; run of records the writer never handed over links to nothing this reader holds, and the framing cannot
; see it; only a head the reader has some independent cause to believe makes deletion detectable, and
; this document supplies the head, which is the same signature the reader is already checking. It is not
; proof of freshness either: an export states when it was assembled and nothing about whether the store
; has written since, and a reader who needs that compares `at` against a clock of their own choosing and
; says so out loud. It is not a statement that the originals are what any law, contract or proceeding
; requires, and the assessment block above is where this container says it made no such claim. And it is
; not a verification of the receipts inside it: an anchored item's bytes are the payload the record was
; hashed with, and the walk hashes them without opening them. A reader that wants each receipt's own
; signature checked runs `verifyReceipt` over the bytes with the key it holds for the kid this document
; names, which is a second check the reader makes and not one this container performed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Closure is one rule across this file and not a rule about one map, as `receipt.cddl` and `pack.cddl`
; state it. No block above carries a `...`, and the absence means there what it means here: a member the
; version named by `v` does not define makes the document invalid rather than a document read with the
; unexpected member dropped. That reaches the manifest, the assessment, the claim, all three collection
; arms, both item rules, both original arms and the signed header alike, and it reaches them at every
; arm a `k` can land on: a member that only one arm of a choice carries is still a member this version
; defines for that arm and for no other, and a reader that read an `anchored` collection as though it
; were a `plain` one would be dropping the two endpoints out of a signed document.
;
; The signed header closes under the reason it closes in the other two files, and it is stronger here
; than it is for a payload: its bytes are hashed into the `Sig_structure`, so a label this block does not
; name is an authenticated parameter, and a reader that took its three labels out of a header carrying a
; fourth would hand on a document other than the one the issuer signed. Its members are the COSE
; registry's integers, which is why they are numbered rather than named.
;
; One map a signer fills at will, and that is a decision rather than a gap: the `unprotected` map of
; `COSE_Sign1-Export-COSE` sits outside the signature, so nothing written in it can travel as a claim
; about the material, and enforcing that it is empty would buy strictness with no security content
; behind it. What the format declares of it is only that it carries no claim.
;
; Every number above written `int` is a CBOR integer, major type 0 or 1, and no other major type carries
; one. A floating-point number whose value happens to be a whole number is a different type, and the only
; reader that can ever tell them apart is one reading the bytes, so a reader decodes every map this file
; names member by member, in a value and in a key alike, where no floating-point number may appear.
; Nothing any of them declares can be a float, so that rule refuses no document that follows this file.
; It matters here as much as it does in a pack, because the quantities this document states are instants,
; and an instant is the kind of quantity somebody reaches for a fraction of.
;
; Sign is the other half, and it belongs to a reader in the same way: every instant this file names is a
; unix time no earlier than the epoch and every count a whole number no smaller than zero, so a negative
; stamp is a malformed document rather than an unusual way of writing a quantity. CDDL writes both `int`
; because it owns no clock, and the floor belongs to the meaning rather than to the type.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
